# What does NOT get uploaded to Vercel.
#
# outputDirectory is "." — every file in this repo that reaches the deployment
# is publicly fetchable. So this file is a security boundary as much as a size
# one: anything listed here is both un-served and un-uploaded.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING content/dist/vectors.json
#
# It is 967 KB, it is read server-side only, and excluding it looks like free
# money. It is not. lib/knowledge/embed.js loads it with
#
#     new URL("../../content/dist/vectors.json", import.meta.url)
#
# and .vercelignore removes a file from the UPLOAD, not merely from static
# serving — so the serverless function loses it too, and functions.includeFiles
# cannot bring back a file that was never uploaded. The two mechanisms are in
# conflict by construction; there is no "exclude from serving, keep for the
# function" setting while outputDirectory is ".".
#
# The failure is silent and total: `cache` becomes null, embedRank() returns
# null, and EVERY search falls back to BM25 — 93% -> 74% hit@3 — with no error,
# no log line and no change in status code. The only visible tell is `ranker`
# flipping from "embeddings" to "bm25" in a search_content result.
#
# Do not exclude it without a post-deploy smoke test asserting
# ranker === "embeddings" against the real deployment. Losing the ranker
# silently is far worse than serving 967 KB.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

# The eval harness's embedding cache: 1.65 MB, and the largest single file in
# the repo. Read by evals/run.mjs alone (evals/run.mjs:302) — a local and CI
# harness that never runs on Vercel. No function imports it, no page fetches
# it; evals.html only names it in prose. Nothing server-side can miss it.
evals/vectors.json

# Internal engineering documents. These enumerate this deployment's own
# vulnerabilities with reproduction steps, and were being served as public
# static files at /docs/audits/*. Nothing links to them.
docs/

# Agent definitions and slice contracts — repository tooling, not the site.
.claude/

# CI configuration. Nothing fetches it.
.github/

# The behaviour suite and the generators. Same category as the three above:
# internal engineering material that nothing links to and that was being served
# as public static files — including test/fixtures/boundary-cases.json, which is
# a list of deliberately-crafted boundary violations.
#
# CHECKED BEFORE EXCLUDING, the way the vectors.json note above demands, because
# .vercelignore removes a file from the UPLOAD and not merely from serving:
#   - nothing in api/ or lib/knowledge/ imports, reads or fetches either path at
#     runtime — every mention of them in those two slices is a comment,
#   - no page or client script references them (no /test/* or /scripts/* in any
#     .html or in js/),
#   - vercel.json sets buildCommand to "", so no script in scripts/ runs on the
#     platform. `npm run build` and `npm test` are local and CI, from the repo.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash: unanchored, `scripts/` would also match
# design-system/scripts/, which is a separate judgement nobody has made here.
/test/
/scripts/

# The second site's SOURCE TREE. apps/next is a SEPARATE Vercel project — its
# own Root Directory, its own lockfile, its own static export — and no file
# under it belongs to this deployment. Without this entry the vanilla domain
# would publish apps/next/src/**, next.config.mjs, tsconfig.json and
# package-lock.json as fetchable static files at /apps/next/..., because
# outputDirectory is "." and buildCommand is "" — whatever is uploaded is
# served, verbatim, with no build step in between to leave it behind. A CLI
# deploy would add apps/next/out/ on top: a duplicate of the entire second
# site, unversioned and silently stale, on the wrong domain. (A Git deploy
# would not — out/ is gitignored — which is exactly the kind of difference
# between the two deploy paths this file should not leave to chance.)
#
# CHECKED BEFORE EXCLUDING, the way the vectors.json note above demands,
# because this removes the path from the UPLOAD and not merely from serving:
#   - nothing in api/ or lib/knowledge/ reads, imports or fetches any path
#     under apps/ at runtime,
#   - no page or client script here references it (no /apps/ in any .html at
#     the repo root or in js/),
#   - vercel.json declares no routes, rewrites or redirects, and no
#     functions.includeFiles, so there is no second path by which a request to
#     this deployment could reach apps/ content.
# The dependency runs the other way and stays there: apps/next reads ../../css,
# ../../content/dist, ../../evals/dist and the design system at build time, in
# ITS build, from ITS project.
#
# Anchored, for the same reason as /test/ and /scripts/: this names the one
# apps/ at the repo root, not a directory of that name inside some slice.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE CONFIGURING THE apps/next PROJECT.
# That project needs "Include files outside the Root Directory" ON — three of
# its four inputs live above apps/next. With that setting on, the build context
# is the whole repository, which means this file is plausibly read by that
# project's upload as well, and `/apps/` would then exclude that project's own
# source from its own build. It cannot be patched around from here: gitignore
# semantics do not allow re-including a path whose parent directory is
# excluded, so a trailing `!/apps/next/` would do nothing. Confirm on a preview
# deployment of the second project before trusting this; if it does apply, the
# fix belongs in that project's configuration, not in a weaker pattern here.
/apps/
